# `Set` infix operators, `Set.empty`, and `s(x)`

The named methods (`.union`, `.intersect`, `.diff`, `.contains`) were pinned by
`tests/conformance/set-distinct.ssc` when Set got a representation of its own. The **operator**
spellings reach a different path on every lane and were wrong in three different ways —
found by measuring them, filed separately, fixed here.

| | `int` / `jvm` | before |
|---|---|---|
| `Set(1,2,3) -- Set(2)` | `Set(1, 3)` | v2: `Set(1, 2, 3)` — **silent**, exit 0 |
| `Set(1,2) & Set(2,3)` | `Set(2)` | v2: `ssc: expected Int, got Set(1, 2)` |
| `Set(1,2)(2)` | `true` | int: `[ERROR] Not callable: Set(1, 2)` |
| `Set.empty` | `Set()` | js: `Method not found: empty on <function>` |

The `--` one is the one to remember. It never lexed on the v2 lane: `xs -- ys` split into `-` and a
unary minus and evaluated to `xs - (-ys)`, which returns the receiver unchanged. That is not a Set
bug — `List(1,2,3) -- List(2)` returned the list where the interpreter raises `No method '--'` — so
a whole operator evaluated to nothing, for every receiver, at exit 0.

```scalascript
// The four infix forms. Each reaches a different mechanism: `++` a concat helper, `--` the
// arithmetic table, `&`/`|` the BITWISE primitives (Scala overloads them on Set).
println(Set(1, 2) ++ Set(3, 4))
println(Set(1, 2, 3) -- Set(2))
println(Set(1, 2) & Set(2, 3))
println(Set(1, 2) | Set(3))
println(Set(1, 2) + 3)
println(Set(1, 2) - 1)

// `s(x)` is membership — a Set[A] IS an A => Boolean in Scala.
println(Set(1, 2)(2))
println(Set(1, 2)(9))

// The empty companion. `Set()` and `Set.empty` take DIFFERENT paths on both fronts, which is why
// both are here: `Set()` is a constructor application, `Set.empty` a companion select.
println(Set.empty)
println(Set())
println(Set.empty.isEmpty)

// GUARD: `&` and `|` must still be bitwise on integers. The fix reconstructs the Set meaning at
// runtime from the receiver, so this row is what keeps it from swallowing the arithmetic one.
println(6 & 3)
println(6 | 3)
println(6 ^ 3)
```

`6 ^ 3` is in the guard set on purpose: `^` shares the emitter path with `&`/`|` and was NOT changed,
so it pins the untouched half of that table.
